<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  exemu's code-signing entitlements (roadmap W9.7).

  Exactly one entitlement, because exactly one is needed and justified:

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit -- crates/jit/src/buffer.rs's W^X code
  buffer calls mmap(..., MAP_JIT). That works unentitled for an ad-hoc-signed
  binary (a plain `cargo build`/`cargo test`), which is why the JIT's own
  tests never needed this file. The moment exemu ships as a notarized .app
  signed with the HARDENED RUNTIME (`codesign --options runtime`), the same
  mmap call fails with EPERM unless the binary carries this entitlement --
  see buffer.rs's "Packaging requirement" doc section, which this file exists
  to satisfy.

  Deliberately NOT used: com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
  and com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection are weaker
  alternatives that also silence the EPERM, by disabling W^X protection more
  broadly than the one mmap call that needs it. buffer.rs's docs call this out
  explicitly; do not add either without a documented reason that overrides
  that guidance.

  No App Sandbox entitlement here on purpose -- that is a substantially
  bigger decision (arbitrary --wine-boot prefix directories, the guest
  sandbox filesystem, real BSD sockets, CoreAudio) that roadmap W9.5 (the
  security model) owns; bolting on a partial, unaudited sandbox here would be
  worse than the status quo.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
